{"tool_name":"gfa","contents":["biotools","oeb"],"identifiers":["biotools:gfa"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"GFA","home":"https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/GFA/index.html","summary":"An integrative machine learning approach to discovering multi-level molecular mechanisms of obesity using data from monozygotic twin pairs.\n\nWe combined clinical, cytokine, genomic, methylation and dietary data from 43 young adult monozygotic twin pairs (aged 22 36, 53% female), where 25 of the twin pairs were substantially weight discordant (delta BMI > 3kg m2). These measurements were originally taken as part of the TwinFat study, a substudy of The Finnish Twin Cohort study. These five large multivariate data sets (comprising 42, 71, 1587, 1605 and 63 variables, respectively) were jointly analysed using an integrative machine learning method called Group Factor Analysis (GFA) to offer new hypotheses into the multi-molecular-level interactions associated with the development of obesity. New potential links between cytokines and weight gain are identified, as well as associations between dietary, inflammatory and epigenetic factors","addition_date":"2020-01-14T20:13:00Z","last_update_date":"2026-11-12T14:21:53.401928Z","tool_type":["Library"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:GFA","edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Essential dynamics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3891"},{"term":"Regression analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3659"}]}],"name":"GFA","owner":"Pub2Tools","publication":[{"doi":"10.1098/rsos.200872","type":["Primary"]},{"doi":"10.1101/2019.12.19.19015347","type":["Preprint"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Epigenetics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3295"},{"term":"GWAS study","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3517"},{"term":"Machine learning","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3474"}],"validated":1},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:gfa/lib/cran.r-project.org","